=== start ===  
# The NPC is watching a televised announcement about the policy in a crowded plaza.

#speaker: Player  
Hey, watching the announcement? They’re saying it’s critical for national security, but doesn’t it feel like something’s off?  

* [Policies like this should be scrutinized—especially when they’re tied to national security.] -> logical_tone  
* [It’s upsetting how they use fear to justify these sweeping changes, isn’t it?] -> emotional_tone  
* [Notice how they never explain how these policies will actually keep us safer?] -> argumentative_tone  
* [It sounds urgent, but maybe we should ask more questions before supporting it.] -> reserved_tone  

=== logical_tone ===  
#speaker: Player  
Policies like this should be scrutinized—especially when they’re tied to national security. Oversight is key to ensuring they’re fair.  

#speaker: other  
I get that, but shouldn’t we trust them to do what’s best for the country?  

* [Trust is earned through transparency. Without it, how can we be sure?] -> logical_tone  
* [I hope so, but doesn’t this feel like giving them too much unchecked power?] -> emotional_tone  
* [That’s the problem—they expect blind trust without accountability.] -> argumentative_tone  
* [You’re not wrong. But a little caution never hurts.] -> reserved_tone  

=== emotional_tone ===  
#speaker: Player  
It’s upsetting how they use fear to justify these sweeping changes, isn’t it? Why do they always rely on fear instead of facts?  

#speaker: other  
Fear works, I guess. People listen when they feel threatened.  

* [But manipulating fear only creates division and mistrust.] -> logical_tone  
* [It’s sad how they exploit our emotions instead of providing real transparency.] -> emotional_tone  
* [That’s exactly what they count on—fear keeps us too distracted to question them.] -> argumentative_tone  
* [It’s true, fear is effective. But let’s not let it cloud our judgment.] -> reserved_tone  

=== argumentative_tone ===  
#speaker: Player  
Notice how they never explain how these policies will actually keep us safer? It’s all vague promises and no substance.  

#speaker: other  
Maybe they can’t reveal everything. Security depends on some level of secrecy.  

* [True, but secrecy without accountability opens the door for abuse.] -> logical_tone  
* [That’s the issue—they hide behind secrecy to avoid answering real questions.] -> emotional_tone  
* [Secrecy is just an excuse to avoid scrutiny. We deserve better.] -> argumentative_tone  
* [You’re right, but let’s make sure the secrecy isn’t being abused.] -> reserved_tone  

=== reserved_tone ===  
#speaker: Player  
It sounds urgent, but maybe we should ask more questions before supporting it. Rushing into things can have unforeseen consequences.  

#speaker: other  
That’s fair. It’s always good to think things through before making decisions.  

* [Exactly. Careful consideration is how we ensure policies are truly for the public good.] -> logical_tone  
* [I just don’t want us to look back and regret giving them so much unchecked power.] -> emotional_tone  
* [We have every right to demand clarity. Policies like this affect us all.] -> argumentative_tone  
* [Take your time to weigh the pros and cons. I’ll support you either way.] -> reserved_tone